    "event": null,
    "id": "2026/robert-ryman-exhibition-tour-with-curatorial-assistant",
    "payload": {
      "art_forms": [
        "art-design"
      ],
      "date_text": "Fri 6 Nov 2026, 18:30",
      "description": "\n  <p><span>Join Curatorial Assistant Amber Li for a guided tour of Robert Ryman: The Real Thing, focusing on some of her personal highlights.</span></p>\n\n<p><span>The Real Thing is the first major UK exhibition of Robert Ryman's paintings in over 30 years. Emerging as an artist in New York in the mid-1950s, Ryman approached every painting as a test to ‘see what would happen’, discovering that ‘there is never a question of what to paint, but only how to paint.’ For almost six decades, Ryman mainly used white paint and a square format to test materials and their effects, producing a remarkable variety of paintings that affirm materials as they are and ask us to pay attention to subtlety and nuance.</span></p><p><span>Presenting over 60 works from the mid-1950s to the early 2010s, this exhibition celebrates an artist who believed the poetry of painting was in feeling, and that painting was best understood though real, in-person experience:&nbsp;‘the real thing…was the only way to do it.’</span></p>",
      "ends": null,
      "image_url": "https://www.barbican.org.uk/sites/default/files/styles/large/public/images/2026-01/Untitled%20design%20%285%29.png.avif?itok=_2Y-luUT",
      "price_text": "£20.50 (£19 + £1.50 transaction fee)",
      "room": "Art Gallery",
      "starts": "2026-11-06T18:30:00Z",
      "subtitle": "Robert Ryman: The Real Thing",
      "title": "Exhibition Tour with Curatorial Assistant Amber Li",
      "url": "https://www.barbican.org.uk/whats-on/2026/event/robert-ryman-exhibition-tour-with-curatorial-assistant"
    }
  },
  {
    "event": {
      "address": "Silk Street, London EC2Y 8DS",
      "category": "exhibition",
      "dedupe_key": "robert-ryman|2026-10-08|barbican",
      "description": "‘You have to see the real thing… seeing a real painting is the only way to do it.’ Robert Ryman believed that the best way to experience a painting is in person. His paintings, which mostly use white paint and a square format, reward close looking. He wanted you to notice everything: the texture of the paint, the brushstrokes, the material being painted on, how it's displayed on the wall. The magic is in the feelings that arise as you attune yourself to the paintings’ similarities and differences, how they relate to each other and interact with the surrounding light and space. Presenting over 60 works made from the mid-1950s to the early 2010s, this major new retrospective of the extraordinary American artist invites you to take a closer look. You may notice aspects of painting you haven’t seen before – and perhaps you’ll leave wondering what else in the world might become clearer if you simply slow down and tune in.",
      "ends_at": "2027-02-28T17:00:00Z",
      "image_url": "https://www.barbican.org.uk/sites/default/files/styles/large/public/images/2026-01/Ryman%20web%20image2.png.avif?itok=7TY54Bgl",
      "lat": 51.5201,
      "lng": -0.0955,
      "price": {
        "currency": "GBP",
        "is_free": false,
        "max": "20.50",
        "min": "20.50"
      },
      "starts_at": "2026-10-08T09:00:00Z",
      "tags": [
        "art-design"
      ],
      "title": "Robert Ryman",
      "url": "https://www.barbican.org.uk/whats-on/2026/event/robert-ryman-the-real-thing",
      "venue_name": "Barbican Centre"
    },
    "id": "2026/robert-ryman-the-real-thing",
    "payload": {
      "art_forms": [
        "art-design"
      ],
      "date_text": "Thu 8 Oct 2026 — Sun 28 Feb 2027",
      "description": "\n  <p><span>‘You have to see the real thing… seeing a real painting is the only way to do it.’</span></p>\n\n<p><span>Robert Ryman believed that the best way to experience a painting is in person. His paintings, which mostly use white paint and a square format, reward close looking. He wanted you to notice everything: the texture of the paint, the brushstrokes, the material being painted on, how it's displayed on the wall. The magic is in the feelings that arise as you attune yourself to the paintings’ similarities and differences, how they relate to each other and interact with the surrounding light and space.</span></p><p><span>Presenting over 60 works made from the mid-1950s to the early 2010s, this major new retrospective of the extraordinary American artist invites you to take a closer look. You may notice aspects of painting you haven’t seen before – and perhaps you’ll leave wondering what else in the world might become clearer if you simply slow down and tune in.</span></p>",
      "ends": "2027-02-28T17:00:00Z",
      "image_url": "https://www.barbican.org.uk/sites/default/files/styles/large/public/images/2026-01/Ryman%20web%20image2.png.avif?itok=7TY54Bgl",
      "price_text": "£20.50 (£19 + £1.50 transaction fee)",
      "room": "Art Gallery",
      "starts": "2026-10-08T09:00:00Z",
      "subtitle": "The Real Thing",
      "title": "Robert Ryman",
      "url": "https://www.barbican.org.uk/whats-on/2026/event/robert-ryman-the-real-thing"
    }
  },
  {
    "event": null,
    "id": "2026/ryman-in-rhythm-sarathy-korwar",
    "payload": {
      "art_forms": [
        "art-design",
        "contemporary-music"
      ],
      "date_text": "Wed 18 Nov 2026, 19:15",
      "description": "\n  <p>Join us for this intimate performance in the gallery by percussionist <strong>Sarathy Korwar</strong> as he responds to the work of <strong>Robert Ryman</strong>. &nbsp;</p>\n\n<p>Robert Ryman’s approach to painting was shaped by his early love of jazz. After moving to New York to pursue a career as a saxophonist, he became a painter, but continued to listen to music as he worked. He saw painting as a form of improvisation, with each brushstroke building on the last like successive notes in jazz.</p><p>Sarathy Korwar is one of the UK’s most distinctive jazz artists, fusing jazz, electronics, Indian folk and classical influences. His work has earned two GRAMMY nominations, an AIM Award and MOJO Jazz Album of the Year. He has collaborated with Anoushka Shankar, Kamasi Washington, Shabaka Hutchings and the Metropole Orkest, and performed at Sydney Opera House, North Sea Jazz Festival and London Jazz Festival.</p>",
      "ends": null,
      "image_url": "https://www.barbican.org.uk/sites/default/files/styles/large/public/images/2026-09/Sarathy_8%20by%20Keerthana%20Kunnath%20%281%29.png.avif?itok=sPyJOcg8",
      "price_text": "£20.50 (£19 + £1.50 transaction fee)",
      "room": "Art Gallery",
      "starts": "2026-11-18T19:15:00Z",
      "subtitle": "Ryman in Rhythm",
      "title": "Sarathy Korwar",
      "url": "https://www.barbican.org.uk/whats-on/2026/event/ryman-in-rhythm-sarathy-korwar"
    }
  },
